  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with a custom-GLSL water scene. It passes typecheck. I couldn't run `vite build` or open it in a browser, so I haven't seen a rendered frame.\n\n- **Waves:** The water is a 300×300 grid with 90k vertices. Four summed sine waves displace the vertices, and the shader computes the normal from their slopes. A time uniform, `waveClock`, drives the animation each frame.\n- **Ripples and lighting:** The fragment shader perturbs the normal with three layers of animated noise, faded with distance. Reflection strength follows a Schlick fresnel term. A sun glint is added on top, and the surface fades into the horizon colour in the distance.\n- **Sky:** A large inward-facing sphere draws a horizon-to-zenith gradient with a sun glow and a bright sun disc. The water reflects the same gradient.\n- **Colour output:** Both shaders decode authored sRGB colours to linear. The app sets `output.toneMapping` to `\"none\"`, so the output stage only sRGB-encodes. This follows the colour contract in the Aura3D custom-shader docs.\n- **Camera and ready flag:** The camera sits at y=3.5 and looks toward the horizon. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after `app.ready()`.\n\nI didn't add an island or any lights, because the two custom materials don't need them.\n\n",
